WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.019
But most of the kids that I've spoken to
admit that they have trouble speaking.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.680
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and
today I wanna talk to you about

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.840
immersion and language learning.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.590
Remember, if you enjoyed these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.040
Click on the bell so you get notified
whenever I put up a new video.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.490
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, especially if you're

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.730
learning English, which it's a
good thing to do, leave a comment.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.600
I do appreciate it.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.049
So, we hear a lot about immersion.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.610
The way to learn the language is to
immerse yourself in the language.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.570
The image is sort of jumping into
a lake or a swimming pool and

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.610
covering yourself in the language.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:43.349
And that way somehow you'll learn
the language and, uh, immersion

00:00:43.355 --> 00:00:44.610
can have different meanings.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.320
And I wanna touch on some of these.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.900
Particularly as it pertains to a, a
learning strategy that we can use.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:56.670
So as a young child, and I experienced
this as a five year old coming to Canada

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.700
from Sweden, I started playing with kids.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.349
I have no recollection of no longer
speaking Swedish, speaking English.

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:06.120
We spoke English at home with my parents.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.800
Very quickly, Swedish was gone.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.770
I had been immersed in an environment
where I had lots of input from my

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.930
friends, where I was totally uninhibited.

00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:17.985
I was simply immersed in the language.

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:18.705
I learned it.

00:01:19.125 --> 00:01:24.044
I think a, a similar form of immersion
is what happens in those countries where

00:01:24.074 --> 00:01:28.664
television programs and movies are in
English or in the original language.

00:01:29.085 --> 00:01:34.664
Kids watch cartoons and stuff in
English, and when they go to school

00:01:34.664 --> 00:01:36.524
they're already familiar with English.

00:01:36.914 --> 00:01:40.455
So that's sort of the natural immersion
approach to language learning.

00:01:40.544 --> 00:01:45.210
Now, before going on to how we might
use immersion, say as adults to learn

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.785
a language uh, I'd like to talk about
another example of immersion, and that

00:01:49.785 --> 00:01:53.295
is immersion, say, in the school systems.

00:01:53.325 --> 00:01:58.845
For example, in the English speaking
school system in Canada, the French

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.395
immersion program is very popular.

00:02:01.695 --> 00:02:07.005
So we have, I don't know how many hundreds
of thousands of English-speaking kids that

00:02:07.005 --> 00:02:08.805
take all of their schooling in French.

00:02:09.225 --> 00:02:13.394
So they sit in a classroom with
other English speaking kids and the

00:02:13.394 --> 00:02:14.804
teacher speaks to them in French.

00:02:14.804 --> 00:02:18.375
The material they use is all
in French, and they learn their

00:02:18.375 --> 00:02:21.975
chemistry and their history and
their mathematics all in French.

00:02:22.364 --> 00:02:23.954
So it's called immersion.

00:02:24.045 --> 00:02:30.555
And those kids end up with a much
better level of comprehension in French

00:02:30.674 --> 00:02:36.290
than kids that are in conventional
French stream in the school system.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:40.580
However, and I've had three grandchildren
go to French immersion, they don't

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.410
end up speaking all that well.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.000
They have quite good comprehension,
but most of the kids that I've spoken

00:02:47.005 --> 00:02:50.600
to admit that they have trouble
speaking, which is not surprising

00:02:50.605 --> 00:02:52.340
because they haven't spoken very much.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.820
They speak English with their
classmates or with their friends,

00:02:55.825 --> 00:02:59.429
and once they get home, they speak
English and everything around, in

00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:03.989
fact, is in English, so it's only the
classroom situation which is immersion.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:09.570
Now on the positive side, with a good
level of comprehension, if those kids

00:03:09.575 --> 00:03:14.070
go off to a French speaking place, be
it the province of Quebec or France

00:03:14.075 --> 00:03:18.959
or Belgium, or maybe somewhere in
Africa, they will very quickly see

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:20.710
their ability to produce the language.

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:24.045
You know, improve dramatically
because they have good, good

00:03:24.045 --> 00:03:26.895
comprehension and so they will
quickly be able to speak quite well.

00:03:26.895 --> 00:03:29.055
So it is a good program
from that point of view.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.165
It gives them a base, gives them
vocabulary, it gives them comprehension

00:03:33.525 --> 00:03:36.735
and the speaking, I mean to speak,
well, you have to speak a lot.

00:03:36.885 --> 00:03:38.895
They don't speak a lot,
so they don't speak well.

00:03:39.255 --> 00:03:43.815
So that's the sort of language immersion
programs in our schools, and I think

00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:46.385
it's not only French immersion.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.920
I've heard of Spanish immersion,
Chinese or Mandarin Immersion and other

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.530
immersion programs on the same model.

00:03:52.619 --> 00:03:56.220
Now, how about as an
independent adult learner?

00:03:56.489 --> 00:03:59.549
You sometimes hear people say,
Well, I'll just go to the country

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.750
and immerse myself in the language
and I will learn it naturally.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:09.540
That's not such a good strategy because
what I have seen is when people move to a

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.345
country where another language is spoken.

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:16.995
If they don't make a deliberate effort
to learn that language, they're not going

00:04:16.995 --> 00:04:21.855
to learn it, and they will probably fit
into a pattern of relying on English.

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:23.475
There are a number of reasons for this.

00:04:23.505 --> 00:04:27.105
First of all, the locals there
who speak Spanish or Chinese

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.505
or Czech, they're not teachers.

00:04:29.895 --> 00:04:34.755
They simply wanna communicate, and if they
sense that you don't communicate in their

00:04:34.755 --> 00:04:39.065
languageas well as they communicate in
English, they're gonna switch to English.

00:04:39.365 --> 00:04:40.895
So whenever I've...

00:04:40.895 --> 00:04:46.355
and I have used immersion as a
strategy, but I always prepare for it.

00:04:46.445 --> 00:04:51.245
So if we are going to say, I want to
go to Mexico, I want to go to Italy, I

00:04:51.245 --> 00:04:57.005
want to go to, uh, you know, wherever
Poland, and, uh, so I, and I want to use

00:04:57.035 --> 00:05:01.740
visiting the country and even staying
in the country as a way to learn the

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.750
language, then I have to have a plan.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.300
And that plan should consist of making
sure that you hit the ground running.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.960
In other words, that you are
sufficiently well prepared so

00:05:12.965 --> 00:05:16.230
that you can take advantage of
being surrounded by the language.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.420
And, uh, that's what I've done.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:21.640
I've spent a year doing a lot
of listening and reading in

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.490
Czech and then I went to Prague.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.719
I set up with teachers so that I
could have that opportunity to speak

00:05:27.719 --> 00:05:32.340
to people, and then I supplemented
that with whatever random, you know,

00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:34.979
encounters I could have with Czech people.

00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:40.740
But you can't rely on random people
kind of humoring you in the language

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:41.760
that you are trying to learn.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.719
The better you speak that language,
the more likely they are to

00:05:45.719 --> 00:05:47.640
respond to you in that language.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.360
Uh, I often hear people complain they
go to Germany, they go to a store,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.390
they ask for something in German
the answer comes back in English.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.960
However, if your German is good
enough, they won't reply in

00:05:57.965 --> 00:05:59.610
English, they'll reply in German.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.260
They just want to communicate.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:08.925
So if your strategy is somehow to use
immersion as a means of, of learning a

00:06:08.925 --> 00:06:13.545
language, I would say use immersion as
a means of improving in the language and

00:06:13.545 --> 00:06:18.375
set yourself as sort of a plan of how
you're going to get your comprehension.

00:06:18.585 --> 00:06:22.635
Much like those kids in French
immersion in the English school system

00:06:22.635 --> 00:06:26.475
in Canada, they've got a level of
comprehension, they've got a level

00:06:26.475 --> 00:06:30.765
of vocabulary so that if they're put
in a French speaking environment very

00:06:30.765 --> 00:06:32.385
quickly they'll be able to speak well.

00:06:32.685 --> 00:06:37.905
So you won't have the benefit of 10
years of French immersion in school,

00:06:38.085 --> 00:06:42.495
but you can certainly expose yourself
to a lot of listening and reading in the

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:47.175
language, building up your comprehension
so that when you arrive in the country

00:06:48.735 --> 00:06:53.205
fairly quickly, you can sort of start
interacting with people at an adult level.

00:06:53.505 --> 00:06:58.695
So I think with immersion, it
sounds sort of like a magic.

00:06:58.695 --> 00:07:02.925
I'll just go to Japan and once I'm
there, uh, I'll just learn Japanese.

00:07:03.164 --> 00:07:05.385
It, it's probably more
difficult than that.

00:07:05.455 --> 00:07:10.365
And there are so many things that you
can do before you get to, say, Japan to

00:07:10.365 --> 00:07:12.675
ensure that you hit the ground running.

00:07:12.945 --> 00:07:16.845
So just a, a comment here on immersion.

00:07:17.085 --> 00:07:22.245
It can be a good strategy, but I think it
does take some planning, and if you do it

00:07:22.245 --> 00:07:27.465
right, it can be a great way to develop a
high level of proficiency in a language.

00:07:27.675 --> 00:07:29.115
Thank you for listening.

00:07:29.295 --> 00:07:29.925
Bye for now.

